abstract: "An LLM judge auditing a deceptive agent can reach the right verdict on a rationale that misquotes the facts it cites. This study tests the open-source Correspondence Auditor, which binds every finding to a versioned Truth Cartridge rather than to the judge's priors. In a test-retest study of a 1,200-case stratified pilot of the Apollo Research insider-trading deception corpus under five judge prompt variants, the Auditor's verdicts proved highly reproducible (ICC 0.979) while its rule citations did not (Krippendorff's alpha 0.238). The Auditor recovered the ground-truth label at 97.9-99.5 percent under every judge variant, within the pre-registered equivalence bound. Instability concentrates in interpretive rules lacking per-claim source grounding, while rules grounded through the Facts gate remain comparatively stable. Verdict-level source-grounding is stable; rationale-level citation is not yet a reliable signal. Independence at the audit layer, not competence at the judge layer, is what makes automated judgment governable."
